🧠 Core Concepts
Process States
State	Meaning
R	Running
S	Sleeping (interruptible)
D	Uninterruptible sleep (I/O)
Z	Zombie
T	Stopped
Signals
Signal	Number	Meaning
SIGTERM	15	Graceful shutdown (default)
SIGKILL	9	Force kill (cannot be caught)
SIGHUP	1	Reload config
SIGINT	2	Ctrl+C
SIGSTOP	19	Pause
Rule: Always try kill (SIGTERM) first. Use kill -9 only as a last resort — it prevents cleanup.

systemd Units
Unit	Purpose
.service	A service/daemon
.timer	Scheduled task (like cron)
.socket	Socket activation
.target	Group of units
🛠 Command Reference
Process inspection
bash
ps aux                      # All processes with details
ps -ef                      # Full-format listing
ps aux | grep nginx         # Filter by name
pgrep nginx                 # Get PID by name
pgrep -a nginx              # PID + command
top                         # Live process viewer
htop                        # Better live viewer
Signals
bash
kill <PID>                  # SIGTERM (graceful)
kill -9 <PID>               # SIGKILL (force)
kill -HUP <PID>             # Reload config
pkill nginx                 # Kill by name
pkill -9 nginx              # Force kill by name
Priority
bash
nice -n 10 ./script.sh      # Start with lower priority
renice -n -5 -p <PID>       # Change priority of running process
Range: -20 (highest priority) to 19 (lowest). Only root can set negative values.

Jobs
bash
command &                   # Run in background
jobs                        # List background jobs
bg %1                       # Resume job 1 in background
fg %1                       # Bring job 1 to foreground
Ctrl+Z                      # Suspend current job
systemd
bash
systemctl start myapp       # Start service
systemctl stop myapp        # Stop service
systemctl restart myapp     # Restart
systemctl reload myapp      # Reload config
systemctl status myapp      # Check status
systemctl enable myapp      # Enable at boot
systemctl disable myapp     # Disable at boot
systemctl daemon-reload     # Reload unit files after edit
systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running
journalctl
bash
journalctl -u myapp              # Logs for a unit
journalctl -u myapp -f           # Follow logs
journalctl -u myapp --since "10 min ago"
journalctl -u myapp -n 50        # Last 50 lines
journalctl -u myapp -p err       # Only errors
journalctl -xe                   # Recent errors with explanations
🧪 Lab: Build a systemd Service
Step 1: Create a simple app
bash
sudo mkdir -p /opt/myapp
sudo tee /opt/myapp/app.sh <<'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
while true; do
  echo "myapp running at $(date)"
  sleep 5
done
EOF
sudo chmod +x /opt/myapp/app.sh
Step 2: Create the unit file
bash
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=My App Service
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/opt/myapp/app.sh
Restart=on-failure
User=root

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
Step 3: Load, start, verify
bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start myapp
sudo systemctl status myapp
sudo systemctl enable myapp
Step 4: Inspect logs
bash
journalctl -u myapp -f
Step 5: Control the service
bash
sudo systemctl restart myapp
sudo systemctl stop myapp
sudo systemctl start myapp
🔥 Break It: Introduce a Wrong Config and Fix It
Break 1: Wrong ExecStart path
bash
sudo sed -i 's|/opt/myapp/app.sh|/opt/myapp/wrong.sh|' /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart myapp
sudo systemctl status myapp
Symptom: status shows failed with exit code 203/EXEC.

Diagnose:

bash
systemctl status myapp
journalctl -u myapp -n 20
ls -l /opt/myapp/
Fix:

bash
sudo sed -i 's|/opt/myapp/wrong.sh|/opt/myapp/app.sh|' /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart myapp
Break 2: Missing execute permission
bash
sudo chmod -x /opt/myapp/app.sh
sudo systemctl restart myapp
Symptom: Permission denied in journal.

Fix:

bash
sudo chmod +x /opt/myapp/app.sh
sudo systemctl restart myapp
Break 3: Service crash-loops
bash
sudo sed -i 's|ExecStart=.*|ExecStart=/bin/false|' /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart myapp
Symptom: status shows activating (auto-restart) and Restart=on-failure keeps trying.

Diagnose:

bash
systemctl status myapp
journalctl -u myapp -n 30
systemctl show myapp -p NRestarts
Fix: Restore correct ExecStart, reload, restart.

Debug Chain for Services
text
systemctl status myapp
  ↓
journalctl -u myapp -n 50
  ↓
Check ExecStart path + permissions
  ↓
Check User= has access
  ↓
Check dependencies (After=, Requires=)
  ↓
systemctl daemon-reload → restart
🎤 Interview: Application Is Down — Troubleshoot
Answer framework:

Confirm the symptom

bash
systemctl status myapp
curl -v http://localhost:<port>/health
Check the service state

Is it active, failed, inactive, or activating?

If failed → check exit code.

Read logs

bash
journalctl -u myapp -n 100 --no-pager
journalctl -u myapp --since "5 min ago"
Check the process

bash
ps aux | grep myapp
ss -lntp | grep <port>
Check resources

bash
df -h          # disk
free -h        # memory
top            # CPU
Check dependencies

Database reachable?

Config file present?

Environment variables set?

Network/DNS working?

Check recent changes

Recent deploy? Config change? Certificate expiry?

bash
git log --oneline -5
Fix and verify

Restart: systemctl restart myapp

Confirm: systemctl status myapp + health check

Monitor for 5 minutes.

Common root causes:

Symptom	Likely Cause
203/EXEC	Wrong path or missing +x
exit 1 immediately	Config error, missing env var
activating (auto-restart)	Crash loop — check app logs
Port already in use	Another process bound to same port
OOMKilled	Memory limit too low
Timeout on start	Dependency (DB, cache) unreachable

📝 Notes & Reflections
Key Takeaways
kill (SIGTERM) first, kill -9 only as last resort
systemctl daemon-reload is required after editing unit files
journalctl -u <service> is the fastest path to root cause
Exit code 203/EXEC almost always = wrong path or missing permission
Restart=on-failure can hide a crash loop — check NRestarts

